  "summary": "AuthManager.createAuthInstance() now passes getAppName() to better-auth as its appName, so /two-factor/enable and /two-factor/get-totp-uri both name the deployment instead of \"Better Auth\". Measured: the triage sub-premise that getAppName() honours OS_APP_NAME was FALSE under objectstack serve, because serve built AuthPlugin with no appName. At the public door, with only the auth-manager fix, OS_APP_NAME=Acme Ops gave issuer=ObjectStack and a reset email subject of \"Reset your ObjectStack password\". So, under the dispatch producer clause, serve.ts now passes appName from resolveDeploymentAppName, the email capability's existing chain factored out unchanged (one resolver, two consumers, same arguments). With both halves the public door answers otpauth://totp/Acme%20Ops:admin%40objectos.ai?...&issuer=Acme+Ops. Enrollments made under the old label keep verifying (pinned end to end). The issuer is read when the better-auth instance is built, so a branding rename after boot reaches it only at the next rebuild while emails follow per send. That divergence is measured and left to the PM in open_questions.",
  "tests": "All at HEAD e7e32f4f. NEW packages/plugins/plugin-auth/src/two-factor-totp-issuer.test.ts (real better-auth via AuthManager.handleRequest, shared createMemoryEngine): the issuer and label prefix equal the configured appName on enable; ObjectStack when unset; get-totp-uri carries the same issuer for the same secret; a setAppName override before the build outranks the configured name; still-works: an enrollment made and confirmed under \"Better Auth\", then served by an \"Acme Ops\" process over the same store, completes the sign-in challenge with codes from the OLD secret and is relabelled with an unchanged secret, and the stored row does not contain the label. The single-file run reports 15 passed: 5 of them are this file's, and 10 are re-registered from the imported impersonation-bearer-rotation.test.ts, as in the sibling 2FA suite. NEW packages/cli/src/commands/serve-auth-app-name.contract.test.ts (unit tier): five rungs of parity between resolveDeploymentAppName and the email chain, plus a comment-masked scan that serve.ts has exactly one new AuthPlugin( call passing appName: resolveDeploymentAppName( with argument text equal to the single resolveEmailCapabilityArg( call. Package runs: plugin-auth vitest 119 files, 2509 passed, 10 skipped, exit 0; plugin-auth typecheck exit 0 (check:test-typecheck OK, the new file clean and unledgered); cli vitest --project unit 258 files, 3777 passed, exit 0 (the integration tier is declared to CI: no spawn entry or integration-tier file touched); cli typecheck exit 0. ESLint --no-inline-config on the 4 changed TS files: 4 files, 0 errors, 0 warnings. Population: eslint.config.mjs globs **/*.{ts,...}; the count is from --format json; type-aware linting is never enabled (config header lines 326-328), so the narrowed run cannot miss a verdict on an untouched file. ABLATIONS (scripts/ablation-replace.mjs, fix committed first, restore proven blob == HEAD with git diff HEAD empty): (1) auth-manager.ts without \"appName: this.getAppName()\": 5 of 5 new tests red, \"expected { labelIssuer: 'Better Auth', ... } to match object { labelIssuer: 'Acme Ops', ... }\", restored to blob a6696db0 == HEAD. The test imports src directly, so no dist leg was needed. (2) serve.ts without the AuthPlugin appName line: scan red, \"AuthPlugin must receive appName from resolveDeploymentAppName: expected +0 to be 1\", restored to blob 121c8125 == HEAD. (3) The same serve.ts mutation was rebuilt into packages/cli/dist (ablation-dist-preflight --absent passed for the marker \"resolveDeploymentAppName(config.email\") and booted: issuer=ObjectStack and \"Reset your ObjectStack password\". The restore leg rebuilt cli, the preflight found the marker present in dist/commands/serve.js, and the tree was clean. A first attempt at ablation (1) was refused by the tool before any write (anchor x8, ambiguous), and a second was refused after the write and auto-restored (the replacement was a substring of the anchor). Neither produced a reading. PUBLIC DOOR (pnpm dev:todo -- --fresh, OS_AUTH_TWO_FACTOR=true, own ports 38731-38733, each process group killed by recorded PGID): fixed build with OS_APP_NAME=Acme Ops gave issuer=Acme+Ops on enable.",
  "mcp_calls": "0",
  "api_writes": "3, all relay dispatches through scripts/pm (POST /repos/objectstack-ai/objectstack/dispatches executed as objectstack-fleet[bot]): (1) pr_create, POST /repos/objectstack-ai/objectstack/pulls (draft), PR 21752, read-back 9421 bytes sent equal to bytes stored; (2) label-write --assign os-steve, POST /repos/objectstack-ai/objectstack/issues/21752/assignees, read back MATCHES; (3) this os-dev-report comment via post-stamped, POST /repos/objectstack-ai/objectstack/issues/21731/comments. git push is not REST and is not counted.",
  "open_questions": [
    {
      "question": "The TOTP issuer reads getAppName() when the better-auth instance is BUILT, while auth emails read it on every send. Measured at the public door: after boot, PUT /api/settings/branding workspace_name=Renamed Co (200) left enable and get-totp-uri on issuer=Acme+Ops, while the next password-reset email read \"Reset your Renamed Co password\". A branding value present at boot does reach the issuer (OS_BRANDING_WORKSPACE_NAME=Env Brand gave issuer=Env+Brand). Should a post-boot rename reach the next enrollment's issuer?",
      "options": [
        "A. Keep the build-time read (landed). A rename reaches the issuer at the next restart, or at an auth-settings change that calls applyConfigPatch; the code comment and changeset state it. Business need: workspace_name is a Setup-exposed tenant setting with a live write route, but an authenticator stores its label at enrollment, so only enrollments made between a rename and the next rebuild carry the old name, and existing ones never relabel under any option. No pull for a live issuer was measured. Long term: a documented divergence, no workaround. AI-error: neutral; the divergence is stated at the read. Startup: zero added mechanism.",
        "B. setAppName() discards the built better-auth instance when the resolved name changes (the invalidation applyConfigPatch already performs), so both TOTP routes follow a rename on the next request. Business need: closes the window in A, which has no measured pull. Long term: getAppName() becomes live on every surface through an existing path. AI-error: the most consistent option. Startup: adds a second invalidation trigger to the auth-instance lifecycle, so every branding write would re-run the whole better-auth composition.",
        "C. A getter-backed twoFactor({ issuer }) reading getAppName() per enrollment. Measured: it reaches only /two-factor/enable; get-totp-uri reads totpOptions.issuer, which the typed options omit, so after a rename the two routes disagree. It also depends on better-auth reading options.issuer at request time, an implementation detail with no typed contract. Long term: a workaround. AI-error: silent dependence on vendor internals. Not recommended."
      ],
      "recommendation": "A, because the consequence is a display label on enrollments made inside a rename-to-restart window, no deployment has asked for a live issuer (startup focus: no new lifecycle mechanism without pull), and B stays available as a small, contract-first follow-up if one does. C is rejected on the long-term and AI-error axes."
    }
  ],
  "out_of_scope_findings": [
    "carrier: none · AuthPluginOptions (plugin-auth/src/auth-plugin.ts) does not declare appName, although AuthPlugin forwards it to AuthManagerOptions.appName by spread. serve passes it through an untyped variable-specifier import, so a typed host writing new AuthPlugin({ appName }) gets a loud TS excess-property error, not a silent drop. Declaring it would widen a published type, outside this Clause-② no PR · noted in PR Acceptance notes, not filed",
    "carrier: whoever bumps better-auth · the @better-auth/core init-options type doc says cookiePrefix defaults from appName and that APP_NAME env sets appName. Both are false in 1.7.3 (dist/cookies/index.mjs:28 uses the literal \"better-auth\"; no APP_NAME read). If a later version implements it, the appName now passed would rename session cookies on upgrade · noted, not filed",
    "carrier: none · plugin-dev (dev-plugin.ts) and the verify harness build AuthPlugin without appName. Neither reads OS_APP_NAME anywhere, so no surface there disagrees with another · noted, not filed"
  ],
